1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:02,700
A robot built to last ninety days on Mars

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:05,240
kept going for more than fourteen years.

3
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:08,039
Why did it survive so long, and what

4
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:09,160
finally stopped it?

5
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:13,240
Mars does not crush machines. It does not

6
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:16,700
melt them. It wears them down slowly in

7
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:19,560
ways you already know from home. The cold

8
00:00:19,580 --> 00:00:22,080
that kills a phone battery, the dust on a

9
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:25,320
windscreen, a tire that finally splits.

10
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:28,760
So could a robot survive on Mars? Could

11
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:29,320
Orbit?

12
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:32,280
In this film, I will show you what Mars

13
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:35,060
does to the machines we send, how some of

14
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:37,020
them lasted far longer than anyone

15
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:39,680
planned, and what it really takes to stay

16
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:43,200
alive there. At first glance, Mars looks

17
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:45,860
like a desert you could drive across. Red

18
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:49,680
rock, rolling dunes, a hazy sky. It is a

19
00:00:49,740 --> 00:00:52,960
trick of the eye. The air is so thin that

20
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:54,760
the pressure at the ground is less than

21
00:00:54,820 --> 00:00:57,560
one percent of Earth's. The average

22
00:00:57,660 --> 00:01:00,440
temperature is about minus sixty degrees.

23
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:03,980
At the first Viking landing site, nights

24
00:01:04,099 --> 00:01:06,000
fell to nearly minus ninety.

25
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:09,340
And because the air is thin and Mars has

26
00:01:09,420 --> 00:01:12,220
no global magnetic shield, radiation

27
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:15,500
reaches the ground. Curiosity measured it.

28
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:19,080
In one day on Mars, the dose is roughly

29
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:21,380
what you would get in three months on

30
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:21,880
Earth.

31
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:24,920
A person would need a pressure suit just

32
00:01:24,940 --> 00:01:25,720
to stand there.

33
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:29,600
But a robot does not breathe, and it does

34
00:01:29,660 --> 00:01:30,580
not get sick.

35
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:34,300
So the thin air and the radiation are not

36
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:37,140
what usually ends a robot's life on Mars.

37
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:39,560
The killers are quieter:

38
00:01:40,460 --> 00:01:40,980
cold,

39
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:42,500
dust,

40
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:43,940
wear.

41
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:47,400
Of the robots that landed safely and went

42
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:50,300
to work, most were stopped by one of those

43
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:53,600
three. And whatever goes wrong, nobody can

44
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:57,000
rush to help. A radio signal takes from

45
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:00,140
about three minutes to more than twenty to

46
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:02,660
cross between the planets each way.

47
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:06,640
Nobody can steer a rover live like a toy

48
00:02:06,780 --> 00:02:07,140
car.

49
00:02:08,340 --> 00:02:10,780
Every robot on Mars has to look after

50
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:12,900
itself between messages from home.

51
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:15,960
Start with the cold because you have felt

52
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:18,380
what it does. Take your phone out on a

53
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:20,880
freezing day and watch the battery drop.

54
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:23,980
Electronics and batteries hate deep cold.

55
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:27,700
On Mars, every night is colder than any

56
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:30,220
winter night in a British garden, so a

57
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:32,320
rover spends part of its power just

58
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:34,720
keeping its own insides warm, like a

59
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:36,840
person burning energy to shiver.

60
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:40,040
That only works while power keeps coming

61
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:40,320
in.

62
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:44,980
In 2009, the rover Spirit drove into soft

63
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:48,060
sand at a place the team called Troy, and

64
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:51,040
its wheels sank. It could not climb out.

65
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:54,060
It could not tilt its solar panels towards

66
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:55,480
the low winter sun.

67
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:58,980
As the days shortened, its power fell.

68
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:03,900
On the 22nd of March, 2010, Spirit sent

69
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:05,200
its last message.

70
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:09,520
Spirit did not break apart. It got stuck,

71
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:11,600
and winter did the rest.

72
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:14,600
Its mission had been planned for ninety

73
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:17,880
days. It worked for more than six years.

74
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:23,200
The cold can be even more direct. In 2008,

75
00:03:23,320 --> 00:03:25,680
the Phoenix lander touched down near the

76
00:03:25,720 --> 00:03:27,920
North Pole of Mars to dig for ice.

77
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:31,060
When winter came, it went silent.

78
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:34,580
Two years later, a spacecraft in orbit

79
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:37,360
photographed it again. Its solar panels

80
00:03:37,420 --> 00:03:39,980
looked broken, most likely bent by the

81
00:03:40,020 --> 00:03:42,800
weight of frost and ice that had built up

82
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:43,640
over the winter.

83
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:46,860
We make one of these every week.

84
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:50,220
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

85
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:54,120
Spirit had a twin on the far side of Mars.

86
00:03:54,580 --> 00:03:57,500
It lasted much longer, and the reason was

87
00:03:57,560 --> 00:03:58,180
the wind.

88
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:02,220
Mars is a dusty world. Fine red dust

89
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:04,360
settles on everything, including solar

90
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:06,860
panels. A panel under dust is like a

91
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:09,480
windscreen nobody wipes. Less and less

92
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:10,480
light gets through.

93
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:13,400
A rover that runs on sunlight slowly

94
00:04:13,540 --> 00:04:16,040
starves. Opportunity should have starved

95
00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:18,820
within months. Instead, again and again,

96
00:04:18,940 --> 00:04:21,899
Martian wind and passing dust devils swept

97
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:24,680
its panels clean. The team called them

98
00:04:24,740 --> 00:04:27,240
cleaning events. Each one gave the rover

99
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:29,340
back its power, and it kept driving.

100
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,920
Ninety days became years. Years became

101
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:34,080
more than fourteen.

102
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:37,720
Then, in 2018, a storm grew until it

103
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:40,300
wrapped the whole planet. The sky over

104
00:04:40,360 --> 00:04:43,200
Opportunity grew so dark that almost no

105
00:04:43,340 --> 00:04:44,860
sunlight reached its panels.

106
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:48,680
On the 10th of June, 2018, Opportunity

107
00:04:48,780 --> 00:04:50,100
sent its last signal.

108
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:52,620
Engineers on Earth sent more than a

109
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:54,400
thousand commands over the next eight

110
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:57,540
months asking it to wake up. It never

111
00:04:57,620 --> 00:04:58,080
answered.

112
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:01,260
What finally stopped the most successful

113
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:04,960
rover of its time was not cold or rock. It

114
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:06,820
was the sky turning off.

115
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:11,140
Why does dust matter so much? Because on

116
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:13,500
Mars, there is no rain to wash anything

117
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:17,180
clean. The lander InSight spent four years

118
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:19,600
listening for Mars quakes and felt more

119
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:22,200
than a thousand of them. But dust kept

120
00:05:22,220 --> 00:05:25,640
building on its panels. In December 2022,

121
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:28,780
it went quiet the same way. Not broken,

122
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:30,500
just out of light.

123
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:33,460
Even a robot that never runs out of power

124
00:05:33,540 --> 00:05:36,700
wears out. Curiosity runs on a nuclear

125
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:39,720
power source, a small supply of plutonium

126
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:42,580
that gives off heat day and night, dust or

127
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:43,320
no dust.

128
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:46,960
When the 2018 storm darkened Opportunity's

129
00:05:47,060 --> 00:05:49,740
sky, Curiosity kept working in another

130
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:52,060
part of the planet. But its wheels had a

131
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:54,940
different problem. They are thin aluminum

132
00:05:55,380 --> 00:05:58,960
made light enough to fly to Mars. In 2013,

133
00:05:59,020 --> 00:06:02,120
the team noticed holes. Sharp rocks were

134
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:04,260
punching through like a tire on broken

135
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:07,264
glass. Since then, the drivers have

136
00:06:07,304 --> 00:06:09,584
planned routes around the sharpest ground,

137
00:06:10,044 --> 00:06:11,784
and they photograph the wheels to watch

138
00:06:11,824 --> 00:06:12,784
the damage grow.

139
00:06:13,784 --> 00:06:15,404
Why not just build the wheels tougher?

140
00:06:16,164 --> 00:06:18,324
Because every kilogram has to be launched

141
00:06:18,384 --> 00:06:21,204
off Earth, carried for months, slowed from

142
00:06:21,344 --> 00:06:23,684
thousands of kilometers an hour, and set

143
00:06:23,724 --> 00:06:26,624
down gently on another world. On Mars,

144
00:06:27,084 --> 00:06:29,204
strength always has to be weighed against

145
00:06:29,264 --> 00:06:30,224
getting there at all.

146
00:06:31,264 --> 00:06:33,064
And then there is the little helicopter.

147
00:06:33,924 --> 00:06:36,264
Ingenuity was built for up to five test

148
00:06:36,344 --> 00:06:40,124
flights over 30 days in air 1% as thick as

149
00:06:40,244 --> 00:06:44,144
ours. It flew 72 times over almost three

150
00:06:44,284 --> 00:06:48,484
years. In January 2024, after its last

151
00:06:48,564 --> 00:06:51,364
landing, images showed damage to its rotor

152
00:06:51,444 --> 00:06:53,764
blades. It could not fly again.

153
00:06:54,624 --> 00:06:56,244
It is still standing on Mars.

154
00:06:57,124 --> 00:07:01,224
So could a robot survive on Mars? Yes. Not

155
00:07:01,264 --> 00:07:04,424
forever, and not alone. The robots that

156
00:07:04,524 --> 00:07:06,804
last are the ones with a steady supply of

157
00:07:06,864 --> 00:07:09,604
power, heaters to fight the night, and a

158
00:07:09,664 --> 00:07:11,924
team on Earth who drive carefully around

159
00:07:12,064 --> 00:07:15,384
every sharp rock. Sometimes they also need

160
00:07:15,464 --> 00:07:18,044
luck in the shape of a gust of wind at the

161
00:07:18,104 --> 00:07:21,604
right moment. That is what Mars teaches.

162
00:07:22,124 --> 00:07:24,864
Survival there is not one heroic moment.

163
00:07:25,324 --> 00:07:28,424
It is a thousand small decisions made from

164
00:07:28,464 --> 00:07:30,924
another planet every single day.

165
00:07:31,884 --> 00:07:34,564
What would Orbit need? Power that does not

166
00:07:34,604 --> 00:07:37,924
depend on a clear sky, a way to stay warm

167
00:07:38,004 --> 00:07:40,564
through the night, a shell tough enough

168
00:07:40,624 --> 00:07:43,424
for sharp ground, and someone at home

169
00:07:43,764 --> 00:07:46,804
watching. What if the real story of every

170
00:07:46,924 --> 00:07:50,324
Mars robot is not how it died, but how

171
00:07:50,364 --> 00:07:53,084
long people kept it going? The bigger

172
00:07:53,144 --> 00:07:55,344
question sits in the tracks they leave.

173
00:07:55,964 --> 00:07:58,744
Every robot that stops on Mars stays

174
00:07:58,784 --> 00:08:01,464
exactly where it stopped, and the dust

175
00:08:01,724 --> 00:08:02,864
slowly covers it.

176
00:08:03,804 --> 00:08:06,704
Next week, how far is the nearest star?
